om2
note SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-only
note instruct.om: instruction-and-function records for training Omega LLM. Every record is an
note instruction and an Omega function that does what it says, in the five shapes of the coding
note benchmark (tools/bench). The instructions are composed from parts ("twice a plus the larger of b
note and c"), so a model can only follow them by learning what the parts mean; the functions are built
note from the same parts and are correct by construction.
note
note   instruct tasks BENCH_TASKS SEED COUNT   COUNT generated tasks on stdout, in the form bench.om
note                                           reads, with the shared inputs of BENCH_TASKS
note   instruct keep GEN ROWS EXPECT           training records on stdout for the tasks of GEN whose
note                                           row in ROWS equals no row of EXPECT
note
note GEN is the output of `instruct tasks`, ROWS the output of `bench table GEN OMC WORK`, EXPECT the
note benchmark's expect.txt. A generated function that returns the same results as a benchmark task
note on every shared input is that task under another wording, so `keep` drops it: no kept record
note computes a benchmark behaviour. A record is one NUL byte, `note INSTRUCTION`, `fn solve PARAMS`,
note the body and its closing `end`, the prompt form of bench.om. `keep` prints `KEEP tasks N kept K
note dropped D` on stderr.
note
note The generated functions never trap on any input: unsigned arithmetic wraps, every divisor is a
note constant above zero, and array indexes stay below n (shapes 4 and 5 are called with n <= 8).
note SEED fixes the output: the same SEED and COUNT give the same bytes.
note Exits: 0 done, 2 usage, 5 a record over 65536 bytes, 6 GEN and ROWS do not fit, 70 I/O.
note
note Strings are built in the arena gs and named by a handle (offset * 65536 + length). tp builds one
note from a template: @ is the string x, % the string y, # the number k. A value generator leaves its
note code in st 3, its wording in st 4 and in st 5 the wording's level, which decides what may be
note built around it so that every instruction reads one way only:
note   3 a single name or number          a, the argument, 7
note   2 tight, no `and` inside            twice a, a squared, the larger argument, the square of twice a
note   1 closed, with an `and` inside      the sum of a and b, the larger of a and 5
note   0 loose                             a plus b, a times b, a plus 3, a divided by 3 rounded down
note `plus`, `divided by`, `the larger of X and Y` and the like take operands of level 2 or 3;
note `twice`, `the square of` and `the remainder of` take level 1 or more; a whole `return X` takes
note anything. Statements that must run before the value (temporaries) go to pre in order.
note st: 0 random state, 1 temporaries, 2 shape, 3 4 5 results, 6 mem fill, 7 stdout bytes, 8 gs
note fill, 9 pre bytes, 10 path bytes, 11 12 the EXPECT rows, 13 the ROWS cursor, 14 kept, 15 dropped,
note 16 17 the pending instruction, 18 the ROWS end, 19 no index tests, 20 inside an array loop,
note 21 the index may be named, 22 the value just made is `X times Y`.

